{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>What is that in your hand? And he said: A staff, etc.</b> Concerning these three signs, opinions proliferated because of the doubt that falls upon them — what distinguishes this one from that one, given that He said, ״וְהָיָה אִם לֹא יַאֲמִינוּ לְקֹל הָאוֹת הָרִאשׁוֹן״ and so on (Exodus 4:8). And what seems closest to me to say regarding this is that the first sign alludes to Israel, that they were at first, in the days of the patriarchs, in their full stature and their dominion, like this staff that stands upright and signifies rule, as it is written (Psalms 110:2), ״מַטֵּה עֻזְּךָ יִשְׁלַח ה׳ מִצִּיּוֹן״ and so on. And afterward, through their sin, they became a serpent, just as the serpent at first walked in upright stature, and by virtue of speaking the evil tongue concerning its Creator (Exodus Rabbah 3:12) it was lowered from the fullness of its stature to the ground. So too Israel were at first in upright stature, and because they took hold of the deed of the serpent — for there were among them informers and slanderers — therefore they became as dust to be trampled together with the venomous crawlers of the dust. And when the cause that had brought about their lowliness has departed, they will return to their station. And this is what it says: ״What is that in your hand? And he said: A staff. And He said: Cast it to the ground, and it became a serpent״ — for by this He gave him a sign concerning the Egyptian exile and its cause: and Israel were symbolized in it as a serpent."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:17.495585622Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}